---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
import { brand } from '../brand'
---

<Layout title={`About ${brand.classLabel}`} active="about">
  <div class="page-head">
    <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
    <h1 class="h1-inner">About {brand.classLabel}</h1>
    <p class="body-2">
      {brand.name} is a fictional evergreen real-estate fund. {brand.classLabel} is a tokenised share class of it, running
      on the XRP Ledger testnet for demonstration.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="note" aria-labelledby="terms-title">
    <h2 class="note-head note-title" id="terms-title" style="display:block;margin:0">Redemption terms</h2>
    <dl class="note-body" style="margin:0">
      {
        brand.redemptionTerms.map(({ term, value }) => (
          <div class="note-row">
            <dt class="note-key">{term}</dt>
            <dd class="note-value" style="margin:0;text-align:right">{value}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
    <p class="note-foot" style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.45">
      The fund owns property. Units can take months to redeem, and tokenising them doesn't change that.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>How dealing works</h2>
    <p>
      Dealing is in two stages. On each monthly dealing day the Register issues units to the Dealing Desk against cleared
      cash, and the Desk sells them on to the investors who ordered them at the same NAV. On redemption the Desk buys units
      back and the Register cancels them. This is ordinary UK fund practice for a manager's box (FCA CP25/28 §3.6–3.7).
    </p>
    <p>
      Live fund tokens on the XRP Ledger today use single-stage direct issuance to investors. {brand.classLabel} shows the
      two-stage model on the same native controls.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each stage needs two of three keys, and the two key sets are held by different firms. Two keys issue the units. Two
      different keys deal them. Every step, including any skipped one, is on a public ledger.
    </p>

    <h2>Dealing suspension</h2>
    <p>
      The Register can suspend dealing for the whole class. When it does, the Dealing tile on the overview
      reads <span class="suspended-word">SUSPENDED</span>, and the Dealing day and Deliver units pages don't propose
      issues or deliveries until it lifts.
    </p>

    <h2>Not an offer</h2>
    <p>
      {brand.name}, its keyholders and its figures are fictional. Nothing here is an offer, a recommendation or a
      statement of performance. NAV and cash figures are illustrative.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
